WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.568
VOICE OVER: This is drone racing

00:00:04.568 --> 00:00:07.973
THOMAS BITMATTA: Think of it as somewhere&nbsp;
between car racing and Red Bull Air Race.

00:00:07.973 --> 00:00:14.800
So it's a three-dimensional track but you are racing&nbsp;
against the clock or racing for a certain number of laps.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:21.780
VO: Agile quadcopters race around 3D tracks&nbsp;
at breathtaking speeds, controlled remotely using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.613
onboard cameras that stream live video to a&nbsp;
pilot's goggles.

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:31.560
THOMAS: We have a lot of acceleration where you're basically instantly hitting your&nbsp;
top speed or within half a second and that top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.347
speed can be a range of 100 kilometers an hour&nbsp;
all the way through to above 200 kilometers an&nbsp;hour.

00:00:36.347 --> 00:00:41.251
VO: Pilots need to be highly trained, have&nbsp;
incredible spatial awareness, and show

00:00:41.251 --> 00:00:47.706
split second reflexes to succeed and that makes&nbsp;
it an exciting challenge for AI.

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:52.668
ELIA KAUFMANN: Most recent advances in deep reinforcement learning have&nbsp;
been achieved in domains that can be easily simulated 

00:00:52.668 --> 00:00:57.960
This challenge requires to push&nbsp;
a real physical robots to its its limits.&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:07.140
VO: Researchers have been training AI to race drones&nbsp;
for nearly a decade but they've struggled to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.686
close to matching human performance.

00:01:09.686 --> 00:01:14.798
The drone&nbsp;needs to understand its environment, 
then it needs to understand where it is on a race track

00:01:14.798 --> 00:01:17.506
and&nbsp;then you need to master planning and control.

00:01:17.506 --> 00:01:21.615
Good commands to send to the motors that make the drone&nbsp;
fly as fast as possible

00:01:21.615 --> 00:01:29.397
VO: Until now for AI to even approach human level required an external motion&nbsp;
capture system similar to those used in

00:01:29.397 --> 00:01:35.183
film production, a process which makes comparison to&nbsp;
human pilots difficult.

00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:38.369
But now there's a new entry in the race

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:44.340
This system - called Swift - is different&nbsp;
it can race at the level of human world champions

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:51.420
using only on-board video and inertia sensors&nbsp;
and computation but first it needs practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.708
lots of practice

00:01:54.708 --> 00:02:01.312
Swift run simulations to find the&nbsp;fastest route 
through each of the gates that Mark out the race track

00:02:01.312 --> 00:02:03.409
this takes Swift about an hour.

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:10.991
ELIA: This translates to about 23 days of 24 7 flying of this AI pilot

00:02:10.991 --> 00:02:13.117
VO: But simulations alone aren't&nbsp;enough.

00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:21.510
The drone has to fly in the real world, and the real world comes with problems 
that the drone&nbsp;has to be able to adapt to as it goes.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:28.760
On board the drone has two bits of kit that tell it where it&nbsp;
is, a camera and an inertia sensor.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:37.466
Together these can estimate the velocity, position and orientation 
of the drone using a method called visual inertial&nbsp;odometry or VIO

00:02:37.466 --> 00:02:42.840
On its own VIO isn't accurate&nbsp;
enough to keep the drone on course when traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.780
at the high speeds of a race cameras just don't&nbsp;
operate fast enough and problems like motion blur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:56.520
cause the AI to drift off its course so Swift&nbsp;
also uses a neural network to detect and track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.020
the location of the gates and give the drone&nbsp;
a much better understanding of its position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.200
But it isn't finished yet.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:13.680
Even with&nbsp;this information there are unpredictable environmental conditions that could throw the&nbsp;drone off course and so Swift flies a test run,&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.620
using the kind of external motion tracking system&nbsp;
we mentioned before to trial its simulated route.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:26.280
Swift can then compare the information from its&nbsp;
onboard systems with the external data and adapt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:32.100
to any differences. Fine-tuning its simulation to&nbsp;
account for variables that are hard to model like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.740
air turbulence or a loss of visual information&nbsp;
somewhere on the track.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:41.735
Put it together and Swift can compete with the best pilots in the&nbsp;
world.

00:03:41.735 --> 00:03:49.260
THOMAS: I did all right it did have some little inconsistency problems and that was usually when&nbsp;as a human pilot you could sort of capitalize on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.720
that and try and make an overtake but if it flew its&nbsp;
near perfect run and you were just a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.400
off the start you were basically just following it&nbsp;
the whole time and I honestly think in most club&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.742
level races with the system as it is it would&nbsp;
actually still outperform most human pilots

00:04:04.742 --> 00:04:06.217
it flies that good.

00:04:06.217 --> 00:04:13.022
VO: It can turn more sharply, race more&nbsp;
consistently, and plan its moves further ahead than humans

00:04:13.022 --> 00:04:18.818
but it's not perfect, the AI pilot hasn't&nbsp;
been trained to recover from a collision

00:04:18.818 --> 00:04:23.740
and it struggles if there are changes to the environment&nbsp;
after it has completed its test laps.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:31.080
ELIA: Specifically changes in the environment in this case means&nbsp;
changes in illumination if we fine-tune our system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:37.260
based on you know environment conditions that&nbsp;
are substantially different to the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:43.620
condition that we then actually deploy the system&nbsp;
we saw a large drop in robustness which means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:48.907
we frequently couldn't complete the lap or that&nbsp;
we yeah that we that we crash into gates.

00:04:48.907 --> 00:04:56.100
VO: even with these limitations the team thinks that this is the&nbsp;
first time an autonomous mobile robot has achieved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:00.910
world champion-level performance in a real world&nbsp;
competitive sport

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:04.690
and they hope that this approach could be applied more broadly.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.907
ELIA: We could speed up&nbsp;
drones that do search and rescue missions,

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:12.075
we could speed up drones that do autonomous inspections of&nbsp;
buildings for example

00:05:12.075 --> 00:05:15.448
because at the end of the day a drone is limited in flight time

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:21.651
which means&nbsp;if you want to make a drone 
as useful as possible you basically need to fly fast

00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:25.140
to maximize its&nbsp;
utility given the very limited battery life.

